<h2>The question</h2>
<p>
  Decision <a href="/backlog/3320/">#3320</a> ruled that diff size <strong>adds reviewers and never refuses a
  PR</strong>, and named <em>scoped fan-out</em> as the replacement for a refuse threshold: every reviewer gets
  the whole diff and full repository access, each is accountable for a disjoint named subset, plus a seam
  reviewer scoped to the boundaries and an omission reviewer scoped to the whole diff.
  <a href="/backlog/3326/">#3326</a> is the build. This topic is its prior-art survey: <strong>how do real
  systems parallelise accountability for a large change, and does splitting accountability while keeping the
  input whole actually find more?</strong>
</p>
<p>
  Reported as mechanism, not reputation. Where a system's behaviour is not established from a primary source,
  this page says so rather than guessing.
</p>

<h2>The finding that frames everything else — two different interventions, two opposite results</h2>
<p>
  There are two things one might call "decomposing a review", and only one of them has a controlled positive
  result. Conflating them is the single most common error in this space.
</p>
<we-data-table>
<table class="data-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Intervention</th><th>What is split</th><th>Controlled result</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Scenario / Perspective-Based Reading</strong><br>Porter, Votta &amp; Basili, IEEE TSE 21(6), 1995</td>
      <td>The <strong>accountability</strong>. Every reviewer reads the whole artifact; each owns a disjoint
      fault class.</td>
      <td><strong>+~35% team detection rate, p &lt; .01</strong></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Change decomposition</strong><br>di Biase, Bruntink, van Deursen &amp; Bacchelli, PeerJ CS, 2019</td>
      <td>The <strong>input</strong>. The changeset is cut into coherent pieces, reviewed separately.</td>
      <td><strong>Fewer wrongly-reported issues; no effect on defects found</strong></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</we-data-table>
<p>
  Porter et al. ran a 3×2⁴ partial-factorial randomised experiment: 48 subjects, <strong>16 three-person
  teams</strong>, two specifications, three methods (Ad Hoc / Checklist / Scenario). The three scenarios were
  Data Type Inconsistencies, Incorrect Functionalities, and — note this one —
  <strong>Missing or Ambiguous Functionalities</strong>. An omission-scoped reviewer is one of the three
  original roles.
</p>
<p>
  Three results carry the argument:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>The placebo is ruled out.</strong> Each scenario group was best at <em>its own</em> faults and
  "generally <strong>no more effective than other Scenario reviewers</strong> at finding faults their Scenarios
  were not designed to detect" — so "the <strong>content</strong> of the Scenario was primarily responsible",
  not the act of having more readers. <em>This is the result that distinguishes scoping from resampling.</em></li>
  <li><strong>Scoping cost nothing off-scope.</strong> "all reviewers, regardless of which detection method each
  used, were <strong>equally effective at finding those faults not targeted by any of the Scenarios</strong>."</li>
  <li><strong>The partition was deliberately incomplete and still won.</strong> The scenarios addressed "about
  <strong>half</strong>" of the faults the checklist covered.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  And two that cut the other way: the aggregation meeting was <strong>net negative</strong> (gain 4.7% / 3.1%,
  loss 6.8% / 7.7% — "Collection meetings produced <strong>no net improvement</strong>"), and the checklist
  method — the industry standard — "was no more effective than the Ad Hoc detection method."
</p>
<p>
  <strong>The strongest replication is null.</strong> Lanubile &amp; Visaggio, with over 100 subjects: "the
  experimental results do not support previous findings that PBR improves defect detection effectiveness with
  respect to nonsystematic reading techniques." Other replications report no significant difference among
  perspectives in detection rate <em>or</em> coverage. Industry counter-signal from Robert Bosch GmbH runs the
  other way — "the overlap of detected defects among inspectors using different perspectives is <strong>low</strong>"
  — i.e. the mechanism replicates on <em>complementarity</em> but not reliably on <em>total yield</em>.
</p>

<h2>Track 1 — Ownership routing: nobody records who approved which file</h2>
<p>
  Five production systems were checked. <strong>Not one lets an approval cover a subset of the diff.</strong>
  Every one partitions the <em>requirement</em> by path and keeps the <em>approval artifact</em> whole-change.
</p>
<we-data-table>
<table class="data-table">
  <thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Routing mechanism</th><th>Per-file approval?</th><th>Partitioned reviewer sees whole diff?</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>GitHub CODEOWNERS</td><td>gitignore-style patterns, <strong>last match wins</strong>; one review request per matched owner set</td><td><strong>No</strong> — the REST review object has no path field at all</td><td>Yes, always</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Google OWNERS + Critique</td><td>Per-directory ownership, inherited down; recommender finds the smallest reviewer set covering all files</td><td><strong>No</strong> — only a private, non-gating per-file "reviewed" checkbox</td><td>Yes — "look at <em>every</em> line of code that you have been assigned to review"</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Gerrit <code>code-owners</code></td><td>Submit rule: every touched file approved by one of its owners; per-file status <code>INSUFFICIENT_REVIEWERS</code> / <code>PENDING</code> / <code>APPROVED</code></td><td><strong>Explicitly impossible</strong> — see the quote below</td><td>Yes, and reviewing only your own files is <em>discouraged</em></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Phabricator / Phorge Owners</td><td>Packages with include + exclude paths; Strong vs Weak <em>dominion</em> for overlaps; Herald adds blocking reviewers</td><td>At <strong>package</strong> granularity (the reviewer row is path-scoped); per-file accept not established</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Chromium</td><td>Gerrit <code>code-owners</code> + <code>set noparent</code>, <code>per-file</code>, <code>file://</code> includes</td><td>No; <code>Owners-Override</code> puts the whole burden on one person</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</we-data-table>
<p>
  Gerrit states the reason, and it is a reason any per-file scheme inherits:
</p>
<blockquote>
  <p>
    <strong>NOTE:</strong> Code owner approvals are always applied on the whole change / patch set and count for
    all files… <strong>It is not possible to approve individual files only.</strong> This means code owners
    should always review all files in the change / patch set before applying their approval. E.g. it is
    discouraged to only review the owned files, since <strong>the set of owned files can change</strong> if
    <code>OWNERS</code> files in the destination branch are changed after the approval has been applied.
  </p>
</blockquote>
<p>
  Google and Chromium landed independently on the same workaround: <strong>state the partition in a comment</strong>.
  Google's guidance is to "note in a comment which parts you reviewed" and prefer "LGTM with comments";
  Chromium's is the single most transferable line in the track — "If you have multiple reviewers,
  <strong>provide a message indicating what you expect from each reviewer.</strong> Otherwise people might
  assume their input is not required or waste time with redundant reviews."
</p>
<p>
  Two more transferable details: Gerrit's UI has a section titled "Which reviewers own which file", and
  "<strong>If no reviewer is an owner of the given file, a red question mark is shown</strong>" — an
  unowned-territory signal. And Chromium's Rubber Stamper "never provides OWNERS approval, by design… If it
  provided both code review and OWNERS approval, that would be an abuse vector" — one actor never supplies both
  checks.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>What does not transfer:</strong> round-robin load balancing (it optimises human fatigue, vacations and
  queue depth — none of which an agent panel has); emergency override culture (it exists because humans are
  <em>unavailable</em>); "one reviewer is usually enough" (Google's median reviewer count is <strong>1</strong>,
  and that economics inverts when reviewers are cheap); and private "viewed" checkboxes, which are meaningless
  for an agent whose ingestion of the diff is provable.
</p>

<h2>Track 2 — Decomposition research measured accuracy, almost never effectiveness</h2>
<p>
  The untangling line is mature and every published number scores a partition against a ground-truth partition.
</p>
<we-data-table>
<table class="data-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Work</th><th>Decomposition signal</th><th>What was measured</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><strong>ClusterChanges</strong> (Barnett, Bird, Brunet &amp; Lahiri, ICSE 2015)</td><td><strong>def-use only</strong>, deliberately: <code>defUsesInDiffs</code> (a definition in one diff-region used in another) and <code>useUsesInDiffs</code> (two regions using an <em>unchanged</em> definition — "changed all the callers, not the callee")</td><td>1000 Microsoft Office changesets: <strong>~42%</strong> had more than one non-trivial partition; 66% of diff-regions land in non-trivial partitions. User study of 20 developers asked whether the split was intuitive and whether it <em>would help</em> — <strong>belief, never outcome</strong>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>SmartCommit</strong> (ESEC/FSE 2021)</td><td>Graph over diff hunks, hard + soft links</td><td>Median accuracy <strong>71–84%</strong> vs ground truth</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Flexeme</strong> (ESEC/FSE 2020)</td><td>δ-PDG multi-version program dependence graph + name flows</td><td><strong>0.81</strong> accuracy vs ground truth, 32× faster than prior SOTA</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>UTango</strong> (FSE 2022)</td><td>Context-aware graph clustering</td><td><strong>+9.9%</strong> over Flexeme, vs ground truth</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Herzig &amp; Zeller</strong> (MSR 2013)</td><td>Tangled-change detection</td><td><strong>7–20%</strong> of bug fixes are tangled; ≥16.6% of files misattributed. Effect measured on <strong>defect-prediction models</strong> — a data-quality result, not a review result.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</we-data-table>
<p>
  <strong>Read none of these as evidence that a reviewer then finds more bugs.</strong> They say a machine can
  recover the author's intended partition, which is a different claim.
</p>
<p>
  Two empirical results <em>do</em> speak to the special seats:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Interface faults dominate — the case for a seam reviewer.</strong> Perry &amp; Evangelist
  (HICSS-20, 1987) counted a real error population: ~36% of change requests touched two or more C files or a
  global header, and <strong>51%</strong> of the single-file remainder were still interface faults —
  <strong>68.6% of the total error population</strong>. The largest single category is <em>Error Handling</em>
  at 14.1% of all errors, itself an omission class.</li>
  <li><strong>Omission dominates the misses — the case for an omission reviewer.</strong> Khoshnoud et al.
  studied <strong>187 missed bugs in 173 merged PRs across 77 GitHub projects</strong>: semantic bugs are
  51.34% of misses, and exception handling is <strong>36.46%</strong> of those; missing-null-check is
  <strong>47.06%</strong> of the analysis-check class. The sub-taxonomy is literally <em>missing code, missing
  method, missing operator, missing cases, missing null, missing directory</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  Also worth pinning: <strong>a whole-program reader catches a class a per-site reader cannot.</strong> Wagner
  et al. compared bug-finding tools, testing and review on the same code — tools and tests found
  <strong>no identical defect at all</strong>, and "all defects found by bug finding tools were also found by the
  review", which additionally produced 17 defect types the tools never did. Every extra catch was
  <em>cross-location</em>: an unclosed database connection missed "because this was done in different functions",
  and 8 redundant <code>if</code>-clauses to the tools' 1 because "the redundancy of the others could only be
  found out by investigating the logics of the program." <em>Caveats: the denominator is the union of all three
  techniques, so this is coverage-share not recall; the review used three developers including the code's own
  author, unprepared, on one project.</em>
</p>

<h2>Track 3 — Provenance of the two numbers everyone cites</h2>

<h3>The ~200–400 LOC ceiling</h3>
<p>
  Primary source: <em>Code Review at Cisco Systems</em>, a chapter in Jason Cohen's <em>Best Kept Secrets of
  Peer Code Review</em> (Smart Bear, 2006). A 10-month study in the Cisco MeetingPlace group, 2500 reviews over
  3.2 million lines by 50 developers. What is actually established, and what is folklore:
</p>
<we-data-table>
<table class="data-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Claim</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>The chart plots <strong>defect density</strong> (defects/kLOC) against LOC under review</td><td><strong>Established</strong> — and density, not raw count, is what declines</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The defect counts come from 2500 reviews</td><td><strong>False.</strong> "we took a <strong>random sample of 300 reviews</strong> and studied the conversations in each one"</td></tr>
    <tr><td>"Defect" means a bug</td><td><strong>False.</strong> "<strong>If the code is right but there's a better way to do it, it's a defect.</strong>" (Hence 32 defects/kLOC here vs 0.353/kLOC for Coverity — not commensurable.)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The inflection is at 200 lines</td><td><strong>As worded:</strong> "Anything below 200 lines produces a relatively high rate of defects… <strong>no review larger than 250 lines produced more than 37 defects per 1000 lines</strong>" — not zero</td></tr>
    <tr><td>400 is a measured threshold</td><td><strong>Derived, not measured</strong> — from a separate 60-minute attention finding: "a reviewer will probably not be able to review more than 300-400 lines of code before his performance drops"</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Declining density means declining effectiveness</td><td><strong>An assumption the authors disclose themselves</strong> (footnote 5): "we're tacitly assuming that <strong>true defect density is constant</strong>." Nothing measured missed defects.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>"70–90% yield" / "density almost zero after 400"</td><td><strong>Vendor drift.</strong> There was no ground truth of total defects, and the book's own data shows up to 37/kLOC above 250 lines. The chapter's actual advice is <strong>100–300 lines in 30–60 minutes</strong>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>A peer-reviewed replication exists</td><td><strong>None found.</strong> Microsoft's measured onset is <strong>20+ changed files</strong> (a different unit and a much later onset); Kononenko et al. find a continuous size effect on Mozilla with <strong>no inflection point</strong> (coefficients 0.098–0.115, all p&lt;0.001, adjusted R² only 0.128–0.173)</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</we-data-table>
<p>
  For context, independent peer-reviewed medians: Google <strong>24</strong> lines, Mozilla 34, Apache 25, Linux
  32, Android/AMD 44, Chrome 78. <strong>Real review sizes cluster an order of magnitude below the "limit".</strong>
</p>

<h3>Coverity's "churn cap" — real, but not what it is usually cited as</h3>
<p>
  From Bessey et al., <em>A Few Billion Lines of Code Later</em> (CACM 53(2), 2010), verbatim:
</p>
<blockquote>
  <p>
    The new-speak term we use internally is 'churn.' … <strong>We try to cap churn at less than 5% per
    release.</strong> This goal means large classes of analysis tricks are disallowed since they cannot
    obviously guarantee minimal effect on the bugs found. <strong>Randomization is verboten</strong>…
    <strong>Timeouts are also bad.</strong>
  </p>
</blockquote>
<p>
  So the churn cap is a <strong>≤5%-per-release stability budget on how much the reported defect set may shift
  between tool versions</strong> — not a cap on code or paths analysed. The causal direction is the interesting
  part: an output-stability requirement is what <em>forbids</em> randomisation. The often-cited "stops reporting
  after N similar defects" is <strong>not established</strong>; the nearest documented behaviour is deliberate
  unsoundness — "in the extreme skipping problematic code entirely."
</p>
<p>
  The same paper supplies the false-positive economics: "more than <strong>30%</strong> easily cause problems.
  People ignore the tool… A vicious cycle starts where low trust causes complex bugs to be labeled false
  positives"; target "below <strong>20%</strong> for stable checkers"; and "<strong>if the first N reports are
  false positives (N = 3?)</strong>, people tend to utter variants on 'This tool sucks.'" — i.e. order findings
  by confidence, not by file order.
</p>

<h2>Track 4 — What shipped AI reviewers actually do with a large diff</h2>
<p>
  <strong>Chunking as a context-window workaround is the norm. Exactly one shipped system publishes a partition
  where each pass owns a named subset and returns a coverage receipt.</strong>
</p>
<we-data-table>
<table class="data-table">
  <thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Context workaround, or disjoint accountability?</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><strong>Qodo / PR-Agent <code>/review</code></strong></td><td>Fully published compression: under budget the <strong>whole diff goes uncompressed</strong>; over budget it drops context lines, strips delete-only hunks and <strong>skips a file whole rather than truncating it</strong>, degrading skipped files to bare filename lists. <code>/review</code> is a <strong>single call</strong>.</td><td>Workaround</td></tr>
    <tr><td>PR-Agent <code>/improve</code></td><td>Disjoint file groups fired concurrently, then <strong>flat-concatenated</strong></td><td>Workaround; no ownership</td></tr>
    <tr><td>CodeRabbit</td><td>Each file summarised and reviewed concurrently, with priority tiers</td><td>Workaround — the documented motive is <strong>rate-limit throughput and queue fairness</strong>. No receipt, no per-call lens.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Greptile v5</td><td>"a <strong>swarm of narrowly scoped agents in parallel</strong>… each agent explores one hypothesis"</td><td>Partitioned by <em>question</em>, not diff region. Slice and merge unpublished — <strong>mechanism not established</strong>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Cursor BugBot V1</td><td>The <em>opposite</em>: eight parallel passes with <strong>randomised diff order</strong>, then majority voting to drop single-pass findings. <strong>Deleted in 2025</strong> for an agentic loop.</td><td>Neither — pure replication</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Google AutoCommenter</td><td>One <strong>file</strong> at a time, truncated on overflow (~200 lines); multi-file changes processed sequentially with <strong>no cross-file reasoning</strong>. All the engineering is post-inference: suppressing comments on unchanged lines took posting rate <strong>6% → 1.3%</strong>; pruning 22 non-actionable rules took satisfaction <strong>54% → 66%</strong>.</td><td>Workaround</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Meta RADAR</td><td>Partitions by <strong>risk tier across diffs</strong>, not within one diff: risk score → percentile threshold → LLM accepting only at confidence ≥8/10 → deterministic validation. 535K+ diffs; revert rate ⅓ and incident rate 1/50 of non-RADAR diffs.</td><td>Disjoint accountability per <em>stage</em></td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Qwen Code <code>/review</code></strong></td><td>See below</td><td><strong>Disjoint accountability, with receipts</strong></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</we-data-table>
<p>
  Qwen Code's <code>/review</code> is the closest published analogue to this design. Under a size gate (≤500
  <em>source</em> lines — gated on source because "the median diff is 41% tests") it fans out to up to 16
  parallel agents, <strong>each reading the whole diff</strong>, each owning one named lens; its three
  correctness agents are <em>procedural</em>, defined by how they walk the diff (every line / every deleted line
  / every cross-file edge) "so their coverage is complementary instead of overlapping." Above the gate it adds a
  territory axis — ~400-line chunks split on hunk boundaries, never inside a function — while 5–7 agents keep
  whole-diff scope. Its stated rationale is accountability, not tokens: "one agent per chunk means
  <strong>every line of the diff has exactly one accountable reviewer</strong>… not a promise of fewer calls."
</p>
<p>
  Two mechanisms to take from it, and one warning:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>The coverage receipt.</strong> "a chunk with no receipt is re-reviewed before the run proceeds — so
  <strong>'no blockers' can never be reported over code that nobody read</strong>." No production ownership
  system in Track 1 has this.</li>
  <li><strong>Downgrade, never delete</strong>, at the verification stage — a finding may only be rejected by
  quoting contradicting code; anything weaker is downgraded.</li>
  <li><strong>The warning: territory alone silently fails.</strong> On one PR, "chunk agents held every one of
  these defects inside their assigned territory and reported none. <strong>What they lacked was not the lines
  but the question.</strong>" Territory and dimension are orthogonal axes and territory without a named lens
  buys nothing. <em>These are internal single-PR anecdotes, not a controlled study.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>
  <strong>The measured LLM-specific result the field was missing.</strong> Sovrano, Bauer &amp; Bacchelli
  (2025) found models are "<strong>lost in the end</strong>" — significantly worse at spotting vulnerabilities
  positioned toward the conclusion of larger files, across XSS, SQLi and path traversal, on GPT-3.5, GPT-4,
  Mixtral and Llama alike. Naive chunking produced "an average recall increase of over <strong>37%</strong>
  across all models" and CWE types — <strong>at a precision cost</strong>, because more chunks means more false
  positives. This is the strongest evidence for the territory half of the design, and it prices it honestly.
  Independently, review-comment F1 collapses roughly <strong>15×</strong> past a ~50-line diff (0.657–0.800
  under 10 lines; 0.043–0.070 at 150–600 lines).
</p>

<h2>Track 5 — Combining disagreeing reviewers: aggregate in silence, never vote, never debate</h2>
<p>
  <strong>Multi-agent debate does not survive matched-compute replication.</strong> The 2023 positive results
  compared against a 3-sample majority baseline with unmatched compute. Since then: averaged over seven
  benchmarks, plain <strong>majority voting (0.7691) beats every multi-agent-debate configuration</strong>
  (best MAD 0.7377), and more rounds monotonically hurt. A five-framework, four-model, nine-benchmark study
  found "<strong>none of these MAD methods achieve a win rate higher than 20% when compared to CoT across 36
  scenarios</strong>". At matched budget, isolated self-correction beats debate on GSM-Hard (61.0 vs 58.8) and
  MMLU-Hard (66.7 vs 60.7, p&lt;0.001) while debate burns 2.1–3.4× the tokens.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>The mechanism that destroys it is conformity, and it is large.</strong> Measured conformity rates
  under doubt average <strong>47.2%</strong> and reach 69.9% for one model; conformity grows with rounds
  (33.9% at one round → 44.4% at five) and with majority size (32.6% with three agents → 69.9% with six).
  Worse, <strong>57–77% of conformity flips are correct→wrong</strong>, and "<strong>stronger agents are more
  likely to flip from correct to incorrect</strong>". A reflection/double-check step cut conformity 69.9% → 35.2%
  — so if any interaction round is added, make it <em>reflection</em>, not debate.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Voting is biased against the correct answer here.</strong> Shared pretraining correlates reviewer
  errors, which breaks the independence assumption a vote rests on: measured, <strong>roughly 1 in 4
  disagreement cases have the minority holding the correct answer</strong>, a ~10-percentage-point theoretical
  recovery margin over majority voting. A meta-classifier over the disagreement pattern reached 81.2% flip
  precision — i.e. the right move is a classifier over <em>how</em> reviewers disagreed, not a rhetorical
  meta-judge that inherits the same correlated errors.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Disagreement is a calibrated error detector.</strong> Davani, Díaz &amp; Prabhakaran (TACL 2022) built
  a shared body with one head per annotator: F1 <strong>59.49</strong> versus 57.32 for training on the majority
  and 53.54 for a fully independent per-annotator ensemble — and head disagreement predicted error, with mean
  uncertainty <strong>0.049 on correct predictions versus 0.170 on incorrect and 0.199 on false positives</strong>.
  Cost: 22.3 minutes against a 20.5-minute baseline, while the fully independent ensemble cost 158.4. <em>This is
  the architecture: disjoint accountability over shared input is the cheap one; disjoint input is the expensive
  one that performs worst.</em>
</p>
<p>
  <strong>The union trap.</strong> Ribeiro et al. ran three analyzers over NIST Juliet — Frama-C precision
  <strong>0.57</strong>, Clang Analyzer 0.16, Cppcheck 0.08, and the <strong>plain union 0.39</strong>. Unioning
  weak reporters onto a strong one pulls precision toward the pool <em>mean</em>. Their learned filter over the
  pooled warnings lifted precision <strong>0.39 → 0.68</strong> while retaining <strong>95.8%</strong> of real
  flaws (false alarms 3942 → 1158). <strong>The rule is union-then-rank-then-filter, never union-and-report.</strong>
</p>
<p>
  <em>A correction recorded because it is the failure mode this programme keeps finding:</em> an earlier draft of
  this survey cited a "&lt;10% pairwise overlap between analyzers" figure attributed to that paper.
  <strong>That figure does not exist in it</strong> — it came from a search-engine synthesis and was passed
  through unchecked. Struck. The aggregate row above is the exact arithmetic sum of the three tools, i.e. plain
  concatenation with no dedup, which is precisely why that paper cannot speak to overlap at all.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Triage disagreement is systematic, and high agreement is usually a narrowed question.</strong> Google's
  FindBugs fixit reached 0.87 consensus over 10,479 reviews — but only after collapsing "Must Fix / Should Fix /
  I Will Fix" into one class, leaving ~27% with genuine residual disagreement. A Microsoft study of 1.5 million
  comments reached Fleiss κ = 0.947 only after collapsing three categories to two <em>and</em> negotiating the
  contested nit-pick boundary. The pattern across both: "<strong>did X happen</strong>" agrees (κ 0.76–0.96);
  "<strong>how much does X matter</strong>" does not (κ≈0.46) — 68% of CVSS re-scorers gave a different rating to
  a vulnerability they had already scored themselves. <strong>Chasing a high agreement number is evidence you
  narrowed the question.</strong>
</p>
<p>
  Google's Tricorder supplies the operational definition to use instead: an "<strong>effective false
  positive</strong>" is "any report from the tool where a user chooses not to take action", enforced numerically
  — <strong>not-useful rate ≥10% puts an analyzer on probation; above 25% it may be turned off immediately.</strong>
</p>

<h2>The distinction the whole survey turns on — <em>read</em> scope versus <em>owned</em> scope</h2>
<p>
  Almost every result above measures the change a reviewer <strong>reads</strong>. A scoped fan-out that keeps
  the full diff in front of every juror shrinks only what a juror is <strong>answerable for</strong>. Sorting
  the evidence by which scope it acts on is what decides whether it transfers.
</p>
