<p>
  <strong>No study measures the separation.</strong> If the size penalty comes from how much a reviewer must
  hold while reading, a shared-context fan-out does not capture it; if it comes from how much a reviewer feels
  answerable for, it might. That gap is why <a href="/backlog/3326/">#3326</a> carries a three-arm measurement
  of its own — unscoped, scoped, and truncated at the same shard count — rather than inheriting a result.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>One further correction, on presentation order.</strong> Primacy is evidenced (Fregnan et al.,
  n = 106: <strong>64% lower odds</strong> of spotting a defect when its file is shown last; hot-spot-first,
  <strong>+23%</strong> comments). <strong>Dependency-optimal re-sequencing is a null result</strong> — Baum et
  al. report p = 0.2587 / 0.1084 / 0.0537, "no statistical confirmation". The usable knob is <em>which subset
  leads</em>, not how the whole diff is sorted.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>And the honest baseline: there isn't one.</strong> No reviewer recommender in the literature —
  RevFinder, cHRev, TIE, WhoDo, WhoReview, Meta's RevRecV2 — assigns disjoint subsets; every one ranks
  candidates for the change as a whole. The idea's only prior appearance is an unimplemented suggestion from
  <strong>8 of 20</strong> ClusterChanges study participants, asking to "allow different reviewers with
  different purposes to focus on what they want." Nothing validates the design, and nothing warns against it.
</p>
<p>
  A consequence worth stating separately: the decomposition literature's accuracy race (0.13 → 0.83 across
  ClusterChanges, Herzig, Flexeme, SmartCommit, UTango, on ground truth that is <strong>synthetic almost
  everywhere</strong> — recombined commits, merged consecutive commits, cherry-pick chains, successors reusing a
  predecessor's corpus) optimises "did we cut where the author cut". <strong>Because every juror reads the
  whole diff, a mis-cut mis-routes accountability rather than context</strong>, so none of those metrics bound
  the risk that actually matters here — a finding nobody owns. Do not import their accuracy targets; measure
  coverage and precision instead.
</p>

<h2>What this repo's own data adds</h2>
<p>
  Measured 2026-08-27 (full working in
  <code>we:reports/2026-08-27-scoped-fan-out-code-review.md</code>):
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>The dial is inert on most PRs.</strong> Over 120 merged PRs the median change is <strong>4 files /
  317 lines</strong>; only 21% change ≥10 files and 4% change ≥25. The cost lives entirely in the tail.</li>
  <li><strong>Directory-prefix cutting degenerates here.</strong> Ten of the fifteen largest PRs put ≥47% of
  their files under one two-segment prefix; one is <strong>100%</strong> a single flat directory, where a
  directory cut yields exactly one shard. Balancing on churn instead takes the largest shard's share from
  <strong>79% to 17%</strong> on the biggest PR.</li>
  <li><strong>Raw churn is still the wrong weight.</strong> On that PR, 74% of files and 44% of changed lines are
  generated fixtures — so a raw-churn cut spends ~2.6 of 6 seats holding machine-generated JSON to account.</li>
  <li><strong>Identifier-only reference tracing misses most of a frozen string enum's consumers</strong> — one
  constant reaches 4 files by name and 18 by its literal value.</li>
  <li><strong>And the measurement that argues against the design:</strong> two runs of the same reviewer against
  the same commit share <strong>zero</strong> findings at defect level (5 pairs, 7 pooled findings, micro-churn
  1.0). If that holds, resampling explains any gain from N seats and the scoping explains no residual. It is
  weak evidence — small n, biased sample — and it is the only local measurement there is.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  Which is why <a href="/backlog/3326/">#3326</a> ships the fan-out <strong>off</strong> (<code>maxShards: 1</code>)
  and carries an A/B of scoped versus unscoped seats, reporting recall <em>and</em> precision per arm, as the one
  acceptance criterion that can fail if the premise is wrong.
</p>

<h2>What argues against the design, kept together so it is not lost</h2>
<ol>
  <li>The only controlled experiment on change decomposition and review outcome found <strong>no effect on
  defects found</strong>.</li>
  <li>The strongest replication of Perspective-Based Reading is <strong>null</strong>.</li>
  <li><strong>More reviewers may buy nothing.</strong> "Two reviewers find an optimal number of defects" is a
  convergent practice across Android, Chrome, AMD, Bing, Office and SQL; at Microsoft "there was only a minimal
  increase in the number of comments about the change when more reviewers were active."</li>
  <li><strong>The aggregation step is where findings get lost</strong> — measured net-negative in the one
  experiment that quantified it. A reduce stage needs an explicit reason to exist.</li>
  <li><strong>Gerrit deliberately refuses per-file approval</strong>, and its staleness reasoning applies to any
  per-file verdict, along with rename/copy edge cases no production system has solved.</li>
  <li><strong>Disjoint reporting destroys capture-recapture</strong>, the only statistical instrument for
  estimating what was missed — it needs overlap between reviewers to work. Whole-diff <em>reading</em> preserves
  it; strictly disjoint <em>reporting</em> kills it, so the seam and omission seats must keep overlapping the
  scoped ones.</li>
  <li><strong>Adding agents can collapse accuracy through conformity</strong> — one model went from 100%
  unaided to 23% with two peers. <em>That study injects static consensus and does not test whether explicit
  individual accountability mitigates it, so it is a warning, not a validation.</em></li>
  <li><strong>More context can hurt.</strong> A deployed system serving ~400M daily users found a narrower code
  slice beat a wider one "due to LLMs' reduced capability with longer contexts."</li>
  <li><strong>Consensus among many agents is worth little.</strong> One recorded case had 80+ agents endorse a
  false vulnerability, "killed only by a single empirical test." An executable check outranks any panel.</li>
  <li><strong>No published experiment tests whether splitting a code diff across LLM calls changes defect
  recall.</strong> The closest is in-file vulnerability localisation. The design is ahead of the evidence, and
  the honest response is to measure rather than to assume.</li>
</ol>
